  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The terminal velocity of a sphere of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;and density&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math>, immersed in a liquid of density&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#963;</mi></math>&nbsp;and viscosity&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#951;</mi></math>&nbsp;is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#963;</mi></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>&#951;</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence, for spherical raindrop,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.</p>",
